---
import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
---
<LegalLayout
	title="Privacy Policy | Subo"
	description="What data Subo collects when you run surveys and polls in Discord, how respondent answers and privacy modes work, how long data is kept, and your rights over it."
>

	<div class="text-08">


	<div class="container container--small">
		<div class="title-box title-box--center">

				<h1 class="title-text heading">Privacy Policy</h1>

			<div class="subtitle-text title-box__text content_box"><p>&nbsp;Last updated: <em>November 24, 2026.</em>&nbsp;</p>
</div>

		</div>
	</div>


		<div class="container container--small">


					<div class="text-08__item">
						<div class="content_box">
							<div class="content-text"><p>ClearSwell LLC ("Company," "we," "us"), located at 1155 N Cedar St, Glendale CA 91207, United States, operates Subo the Survey Bot and associated websites and services ("Service"). This Privacy Policy explains what personal data we collect, how we use it, and what rights you have. It applies to Discord server owners and authorized creators who manage surveys, and to participants who respond to them.</p>
</div>
						</div>
					</div>


					<div class="text-08__item" id="changes-2026-11-24">
						<h3 class="def-18_72 title-text--inner text--08__heading">What Changed on November 24, 2026
							<div class="text--08__icon">
							</div>
						</h3>
						<div class="content_box">
							<div class="content-text"><p>This update describes data retention rules that now run in Subo, and corrects parts of the previous version that were wrong. In short:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Inactive communities are deleted.</strong> A community's surveys, responses, XP and settings are deleted after 180 days without activity once Subo has been removed from its server, or after 24 months without activity while Subo is still installed. Admins are told 30, 7 and 1 days before, and any activity keeps everything. Communities with a paid plan are not affected.</li>
<li><strong>Server owners can delete their community's data</strong> from the Settings page of the web app, with 7 days to change their mind.</li>
<li><strong>Your data has end dates.</strong> Your membership and XP in a community are deleted 12 months after you leave it, and your identity is removed after 3 years without using Subo. Activity logs and records of the messages we send are kept 24 months. Full details are in "Data Retention" below.</li>
<li><strong>Full Anonymous, described correctly.</strong> The previous version said Subo keeps no link between an anonymous answer and the person who gave it. That was wrong: Subo keeps that link, and never shows it to anyone in the community, the survey's creator and admins included. See "Survey Anonymity Modes" for why.</li>
<li><strong>Your rights, in one place.</strong> You can review, export and delete your answers, or delete your whole account, on your account page at <a href="https://app.subo.gg/app/account" target="_blank">app.subo.gg/app/account</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Removed:</strong> passages about sharing data with research clients and about digital fingerprinting. Subo never did either.</li>
</ul>
</div>
						</div>
					</div>


					<div class="text-08__item">
						<h3 class="def-18_72 title-text--inner text--08__heading">Who This Policy Applies To
							<div class="text--08__icon">
							</div>
						</h3>
						<div class="content_box">
							<div class="content-text"><p>The data relationship differs by role:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Creators</strong> (server owners and admins): access Subo via Discord commands and the web admin. For survey results and response data collected on their behalf, Subo acts as a <strong>data processor</strong> and creators are the <strong>data controllers</strong>. Creators are responsible for the lawful collection of their community members' survey responses.</li>
<li><strong>Participants</strong> (survey respondents): respond to surveys invited by creators. For identity data, XP, and participation history, Subo acts as the <strong>data controller</strong>. For response content collected on behalf of a creator, Subo acts as a <strong>data processor</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you are a participant and have questions about how a specific survey handles your data, please contact the server admin who ran the survey.</p>
</div>
						</div>
					</div>


					<div class="text-08__item">
						<h3 class="def-18_72 title-text--inner text--08__heading">Personal Data We Collect
							<div class="text--08__icon">
							</div>
						</h3>
						<div class="content_box">
							<div class="content-text"><p>We are the controller and responsible for your data. Here is the Personal Data we collect:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Identity data from Discord</strong>: by accessing and using the Service, we will receive from Discord your Discord ID, username, avatar, and the name and identifier of the Discord server you come from. This information will be associated with a unique identifier that we generate for you. You may also be asked to provide Personal Data such as your email address, age, gender, language or location.</li>
<li><strong>Account and login data</strong>: when a server owner or admin signs in to the Subo web app with Discord, or installs Subo from it, Discord shares your email address, whether Discord has verified that address, and the language preference set on your Discord account. The address is kept on your Discord identity record. It becomes the address we write to only if Discord has verified it and you have not already set a contact address yourself: one you enter on your account page is a deliberate choice and always takes precedence. We also store the access tokens Discord issues at sign-in, encrypted, so the web app can re-check which servers you administer without asking you to sign in again. Your language preference is used to show Subo to you in your own language. Members who only answer surveys are not asked for an email address at sign-in.</li>
<li><strong>Other accounts you sign in with (Google, YouTube, Twitch, Steam)</strong>: some surveys ask you to sign in with one of these accounts, either to answer on the web or to prove that you belong to a creator's audience. We receive and keep the account's identifier, display name and avatar, and, for Google, your email address. In Transparent and Semi-private surveys, the survey's creator and the community's admins can see the name of the account you answered with.</li>
<li><strong>Audience checks</strong>: when a survey is limited to a creator's audience, we check whether you subscribe to their YouTube channel, follow their Twitch channel or own their game on Steam. We record the result and, where the platform provides it, the date you subscribed or started following, and we treat the result as valid for 30 days before checking again. Your Google or Twitch access is used for that check only: we do not keep your access tokens. Steam shares no token at all; we read your game library through Steam's Web API, which works only when your game details are public.</li>
<li><strong>Channels creators connect</strong>: when a creator connects their own YouTube or Twitch channel as an audience source, we keep the channel's identifier and name and the access tokens the platform issues, encrypted, so that Subo can check who belongs to that audience. For YouTube we ask only for read-only access (permission to view the YouTube account); for Twitch, only to identify the account.</li>
<li><strong>Google user data</strong>: Subo's use and transfer to any other app of information received from Google APIs adheres to the <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy" target="_blank">Google API Services User Data Policy</a>, including the Limited Use requirements. We use Google and YouTube data only to sign you in and to run the audience checks described above. We do not sell it, use it for advertising, or send it to AI providers, and nobody at Subo reads it except to provide support you ask for, for security, or to comply with the law.</li>
<li><strong>Survey data</strong>: when you create a questionnaire on our platform, we collect the content of the questionnaire and its attributes. We also collect metadata such as the date and time of creation.</li>
<li><strong>Respondent Data</strong>: when you participate and answer questions from our surveys, we will also collect your answers such as personal opinions on the topic of the research (products and services, brands and companies), declared behaviors, demographic information (including marital status, family details, occupation, education, media usage, income bracket, home ownership). In some cases, you may be asked to voluntarily provide Personal Data which may be considered "sensitive", including racial and ethnic origin, political and religious beliefs, sexual orientation and health or disability status. You may refuse to answer these or any other questions or discontinue participation at any time.</li>
<li><strong>Web Survey Data</strong>: if you respond to a survey through the web interface rather than Discord, we may collect additional technical data including your IP address, browser type, device type, and session identifiers for access control, fraud prevention, and service operation.</li>
</ul>
</div>
						</div>
					</div>


					<div class="text-08__item">
						<h3 class="def-18_72 title-text--inner text--08__heading">Survey Anonymity Modes
							<div class="text--08__icon">
							</div>
						</h3>
						<div class="content_box">
							<div class="content-text"><p>The anonymity mode for each survey is set by the creator. There are three modes:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Transparent</strong>: individual votes and answers can be seen by other members of the community in the results, as well as by the survey's creator and the community's admins. Exports include each participant's Discord identifier.</li>
<li><strong>Semi-private</strong>: individual votes and answers, and who gave them, can be seen only by the survey's creator and the community's admins. They are not shown to other members. Exports include each participant's Discord identifier.</li>
<li><strong>Anonymous (Full Anonymous)</strong>: nobody in the community, the creator and admins included, can see who gave which answer: results show answers without names, and exports contain no Discord identifier. This mode cannot be switched off once the first person has answered. Subo itself keeps the link between an answer and the account that gave it, and never shows it to the community. We keep it so that you can delete your own answers, so that a survey can count one response per person and award XP, and so that a follow-up survey can reach the same participants, for example in a later wave of the same study. Anonymous answers may be sent to an AI provider for Text Analysis (see "AI Features and AI Providers" below); no identity information is included in those requests.</li>
</ul>
<p>Participants should contact the server admin if uncertain about how a specific survey handles their data, as the anonymity mode is determined by the creator.</p>
</div>
						</div>
					</div>


					<div class="text-08__item">
						<h3 class="def-18_72 title-text--inner text--08__heading">How We Use Your Personal Data
							<div class="text--08__icon">
							</div>
						</h3>
						<div class="content_box">
							<div class="content-text"><p>We use the data you share with us to run the Service for creators and their communities. We do not sell survey data or Personal Data to anyone.</p>
<p>We use Personal Data to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Deliver surveys and their results to the creators who run them, in the anonymity mode they chose (see "Survey Anonymity Modes" above).</li>
<li>Check who may answer a survey and prevent abuse: creators can limit a survey to members with certain Discord roles or to people who connect an account (for example a Steam account that owns a game), and web sessions use the technical data described above.</li>
<li>Manage your account (if you contact us, we can associate your user identifier to your account details to better support you).</li>
<li>Notifications and service messages: we write to creators about their own account and communities. Messages about your account itself, such as a project that failed to open, an export that is ready, or a subscription that needs attention, are transactional and are always sent. Product updates, tips and occasional check-ins, and invitations to help with Subo's own research, are optional: you can turn either category off at any time on your account page at <a href="https://app.subo.gg/app/account" target="_blank">app.subo.gg/app/account</a>. These messages reach you on whichever surface we can use, which may be Discord, email, or the notifications area of the web app.</li>
<li>Establish a leaderboard ranking different users for the servers you belong to. XP tracking requires participation history; monthly leaderboard scores reset but the underlying history is kept for as long as described in "Data Retention" below.</li>
<li>Study how Subo is used: when a community's data is deleted, we keep a de-identified copy of its surveys' questions and answer options (never the answers) to understand what kinds of questions communities ask. See "Data Retention" below.</li>
<li>Recurring and scheduled surveys: automated delivery on creator-configured schedules. Participation remains voluntary. Response data is handled identically to single-instance surveys.</li>
<li>Creator Network: as part of our own market research, we may contact Subo survey creators (server owners) to invite them to participate in surveys conducted by Subo/ClearSwell. This uses contact information (Discord username, server association) already held as part of your creator account. You may opt out at any time on your account page at <a href="https://app.subo.gg/app/account" target="_blank">app.subo.gg/app/account</a>, or by contacting hello@subo.ai.</li>
<li>Service providers: we may work with trusted service providers to help us run our business, for example cloud data storage, survey/data collection technology, validation and anti-fraud services.</li>
<li>Uploaded images: when a creator uploads an image to a project (for example a poll image, an invitation image or an achievement badge), the file is stored with Cloudflare and served through Cloudflare from Subo's own media domain. Embedded metadata, such as the GPS location a phone camera records, is removed on upload, and photos are resized. We record which community the image belongs to and who uploaded it. When someone views the image, their browser or Discord app requests it from Cloudflare, which receives the usual request data, such as the IP address. <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" target="_blank">Cloudflare's privacy policy</a>.</li>
<li>Files sent by respondents: when a project asks for a file (for example a screenshot, a document or a video), the files a respondent sends are stored privately with Cloudflare, separately from the images used in projects. They have no public address: only the community's admins and the project's creator can open them, after signing in to Subo. Photos up to about 15 MB sent on the web, or 20 MB in Discord, are re-saved without their embedded metadata, such as the camera details and GPS location a phone records; larger files are stored as sent. Files are deleted when their response is deleted, including when the project is deleted or the community's data is deleted (see "Data Retention").</li>
<li>AI features: some features use AI models from OpenAI, Anthropic and Google, which we reach through OpenRouter. See "AI Features and AI Providers" below for what each feature sends, and how long each provider keeps it.</li>
<li>Payment processing (Stripe): subscription payments are processed by Stripe, Inc. Subo does not store full payment card details. We receive subscription status (tier, renewal date, server association) from Stripe, and Stripe calculates and collects sales tax and VAT where it applies, using your billing address. <a href="https://stripe.com/privacy" target="_blank">Stripe's privacy policy</a> applies to payment data.</li>
<li>We may also provide links to third-party bots, websites and apps, which are independent from Subo and have their own separate privacy policies. We are not responsible for the privacy practices or content of such third-party websites or apps. We recommend that you carefully read the privacy policies of each site or app.</li>
<li>In rare instances, we may be required by law to share Personal Data with public authorities, or we may share data to defend our rights, including with legal advisors.</li>
<li>If our business gets acquired, merges, or in case of bankruptcy, your data will be considered an asset and may be transferred to the acquirer. In this case, we will require the other party to agree in writing to respect this Privacy Policy.</li>
</ul>
</div>
						</div>
					</div>


					<div class="text-08__item">
						<h3 class="def-18_72 title-text--inner text--08__heading">AI Features and AI Providers
							<div class="text--08__icon">
							</div>
						</h3>
						<div class="content_box">
							<div class="content-text"><p>Some Subo features use large language models ("AI"). We send these requests through <strong>OpenRouter</strong> (OpenRouter, Inc., United States), a service that passes each request to the provider running the model. The model providers we currently use are <strong>OpenAI</strong>, <strong>Anthropic</strong> and <strong>Google</strong>. A model may also be served by a cloud company that hosts it for its developer or for OpenRouter, such as Microsoft Azure, Amazon Web Services or Google Cloud. We use these providers only through their business APIs, under terms in which they do not use the data we send to train their models.</p>
<p><strong>What each feature sends:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Text Analysis (AI summaries of open-ended answers)</strong>: the question and the open-text answers to it. Before anything is sent, Subo removes the personal data it can detect (such as email addresses, phone numbers, IP addresses, bank, card and crypto wallet numbers, Discord handles, and the names of respondents) and replaces each respondent with a random code, so no usernames or Discord IDs are sent. A question that asks for personal data is not summarized unless the creator turns it on, and when many of a question's answers consist mostly of personal data, none of its answers are sent. Creators can turn Text Analysis off for their whole community, for a project, or for a single question. This applies in every anonymity mode, including Full Anonymous.</li>
<li><strong>Drafting</strong> (the <code>/draft</code> command, "Let Subo draft it" in the web app, and project generation through the Subo API and its MCP connection): what the creator asks for, their answers to Subo's follow-up questions, any facts they provide, and the name and language of their community. No survey responses are sent.</li>
<li><strong>Audit</strong> (a review of a project's questions for personal data, privacy settings and question types, run by the creator or when a project opens): the project's questions and answer options, and its privacy and delivery settings. No survey responses are sent.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>What the providers keep:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>OpenRouter</strong> does not store the content of our requests or the AI's replies. It keeps request metadata, such as the model used, the number of tokens and the time taken. <a href="https://openrouter.ai/privacy" target="_blank">OpenRouter's privacy policy</a>.</li>
<li><strong>OpenAI</strong> does not use API data to train its models, and keeps it for up to 30 days for abuse and misuse monitoring. <a href="https://openai.com/policies/api-data-usage-policies" target="_blank">How the OpenAI API uses data</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Anthropic</strong> does not use API data to train its models by default, and deletes it within 30 days, except where a request is flagged for a usage policy violation. <a href="https://privacy.claude.com/en/articles/7996866-how-long-do-you-store-my-organization-s-data" target="_blank">How long Anthropic keeps API data</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Google</strong> does not use paid Gemini API prompts or responses to improve its products, and keeps them for up to 55 days, solely to detect and prevent abuse. <a href="https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/terms" target="_blank">Gemini API terms</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>What Subo keeps:</strong> AI summaries are stored with the project's results. A drafting session (the request, Subo's questions and the creator's answers) is kept for 7 days when no project was created (30 days on paid plans), or for 90 days when it produced a project (18 months on paid plans). Audit results are stored with the project and deleted with it. For every AI request we also record the model, the provider, the number of tokens and the cost.</p>
</div>
						</div>
					</div>


					<div class="text-08__item" id="data-retention">
						<h3 class="def-18_72 title-text--inner text--08__heading">Data Retention
							<div class="text--08__icon">
							</div>
						</h3>
						<div class="content_box">
							<div class="content-text">
<p>We keep data only as long as it serves the community it belongs to or the person it is about. The rules below run automatically every night.</p>
<p><strong>Inactive communities.</strong> When a community has had no activity (no new survey, no answers, no admin using the web app) for <strong>180 days after Subo was removed from its server</strong>, or for <strong>24 months while Subo is still installed</strong>, we delete its data: surveys, responses, members' XP and achievements, and settings. Its admins are told 30, 7 and 1 days before, in Discord or by email, and any activity before the date keeps everything as it is. When Subo has been removed and we have no way to reach any of its admins, the data is deleted without notice once the 180 days have passed. A community with a paid plan is never deleted for inactivity.</p>
<p><strong>Deleting a community's data on request.</strong> The owner of a Discord server can ask us to delete the community's data from the Settings page of the web app. It is deleted 7 days later, with the same steps as an inactive community, and Subo then leaves the server. The community's admins are told right away and the day before, and the owner can cancel until then. This is not available while the community has a paid plan.</p>
<p><strong>What stays after a community's data is deleted.</strong> A small record without any names: the server's identifier, the dates Subo was installed and removed, a few technical settings and its credit balance, the number of surveys, responses and participants it had, and its billing records. Once Subo is no longer in the server, this record is deleted 3 years after the community's data, or 10 years after if the community paid for a plan (tax records). Only anonymous totals per month remain after that. We also keep a de-identified copy of the deleted surveys' questions and answer options for 5 years, with no answers, no identifiers and no names: mentions, links, email addresses and the community's own name are removed from the text.</p>
<p><strong>Your account.</strong> You can delete your whole Subo account at any time on your account page (see "Your Rights"). It is immediate and covers every community, including your anonymous answers. If you have not used Subo for <strong>3 years</strong>, we remove your identity: your Discord identifier, username, avatar, email addresses, connected accounts, memberships and XP. Your answers stay with the communities that collected them, without anything that identifies you, and if you come back you start with a new account. This does not apply while you pay for a plan, help run an active community, or own an API key or webhook.</p>
<p><strong>Backups.</strong> We keep encrypted daily backups of our database for 7 days, so data deleted under these rules may remain in a backup for up to 7 days.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Data type</th><th>Retention</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Survey responses</td><td>Kept while the community uses Subo. Deleted with the survey, when a creator on a paid plan deletes them, when you delete your answers on your account page, when you delete your account, or when the community's data is deleted (inactive community or owner's request)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Membership, XP and achievements in a community</td><td>Kept while you are a member; deleted 12 months after you leave the community (your answers stay), or with your account. Monthly leaderboard scores reset, the underlying history does not</td></tr>
<tr><td>Identity data (Discord ID, username, avatar)</td><td>Until you delete your account; identity removed after 3 years without activity (see above)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Account login data (email address, language preference, encrypted Discord access tokens)</td><td>Retained while the account exists; tokens are replaced at each sign-in and deleted with the account</td></tr>
<tr><td>Other accounts you sign in with (Google, YouTube, Twitch, Steam: identifier, name, avatar, Google email)</td><td>Kept while your Subo account exists; deleted with your account, or when your identity is removed after 3 years without activity</td></tr>
<tr><td>Audience checks (subscribes, follows or owns the game, and since when)</td><td>Valid for 30 days, then checked again; deleted with your account or identity</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tokens of a channel a creator connects (YouTube, Twitch)</td><td>Encrypted; kept with the creator's Subo account and deleted with it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Community data (surveys, responses, members' XP, settings)</td><td>Deleted after 180 days without activity once Subo was removed, after 24 months without activity while Subo is installed, or 7 days after the owner asks (see above)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Record of a deleted community (no names)</td><td>3 years after the deletion, 10 years if the community paid; then anonymous monthly totals only</td></tr>
<tr><td>De-identified questions of deleted surveys (no answers)</td><td>5 years</td></tr>
<tr><td>Activity log (what was done in Subo, by whom and when)</td><td>24 months; after that, only a summary per person and community: the first and last time, and how many times</td></tr>
<tr><td>Messages we sent you (who, where and when)</td><td>24 months; for notices about a community's data deletion, 30 days after the deletion</td></tr>
<tr><td>Report files generated for download</td><td>Deleted within 24 hours</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI requests at OpenRouter</td><td>Content not stored; request metadata (model, tokens, timing) kept per OpenRouter's policy</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI requests at the model providers</td><td>OpenAI: up to 30 days; Anthropic: deleted within 30 days (longer only for requests flagged for policy violations); Google: up to 55 days. Abuse monitoring only; no model training</td></tr>
<tr><td>Drafting sessions</td><td>7 days if no project was created (30 days on paid plans); 90 days if a project was created (18 months on paid plans)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Files sent by respondents</td><td>Deleted with their response (response, project, test-answer, account or community data deletion), within a day</td></tr>
<tr><td>Uploaded images</td><td>Deleted automatically about 7 days after no project uses them (the project was deleted, or the image was replaced or removed)</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI summaries and audit results</td><td>Stored with the project and deleted with it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Billing data</td><td>Stripe keeps payment data per Stripe's policy and applicable financial law. Our own subscription records are kept with the community, and for 10 years after its data is deleted</td></tr>
<tr><td>Backups</td><td>Encrypted daily backups kept 7 days</td></tr>
<tr><td>Google Analytics data</td><td>Per GA4 data retention settings (configurable in GA4 Admin &gt; Data Settings)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
						</div>
					</div>


					<div class="text-08__item">
						<h3 class="def-18_72 title-text--inner text--08__heading">Your Rights
							<div class="text--08__icon">
							</div>
						</h3>
						<div class="content_box">
							<div class="content-text"><p>You have the right to request access to Personal Data, the correction or deletion of any Personal Data we hold about you.</p>
<p>You can do most of this yourself on your account page at <a href="https://app.subo.gg/app/account" target="_blank">app.subo.gg/app/account</a> (the <code>/profile</code> command in Discord links there), after signing in with Discord:</p>
<ul>
<li>review and export the answers you gave in each community, and delete some or all of them, anonymous answers included;</li>
<li>delete your whole Subo account, in every community, immediately. If you pay for a Subo subscription, cancel it first, so the community it pays for does not lose its plan without notice;</li>
<li>choose which messages you receive from us and from your communities.</li>
</ul>
<p>The owner of a Discord server can also delete all of the community's data from the Settings page of the web app (see "Data Retention"). If you are unable to perform these actions yourself, please contact us to assist you at hello@subo.ai.</p>
<p>In addition, you can also simply exercise your rights by contacting us at hello@subo.ai. To protect your privacy and security, we take reasonable measures to verify your identity before granting you such access.</p>
<p>You also have the right to be free from discrimination for exercising your rights.</p>
</div>
						</div>
					</div>


					<div class="text-08__item">
						<h3 class="def-18_72 title-text--inner text--08__heading">EEA and UK Residents
							<div class="text--08__icon">
							</div>
						</h3>
						<div class="content_box">
							<div class="content-text"><p>If you are a resident of the European Economic Area (EEA) or United Kingdom, you may have certain data protection rights granted by the GDPR and UK GDPR.</p>
<p><strong>Your GDPR rights include:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The right to access, rectify or delete</strong> the information we have on you.</li>
<li><strong>The right to data portability</strong>: the right to receive a copy of your data in a structured, machine-readable format.</li>
<li><strong>The right to appeal automated decision-making</strong>: the right to request human intervention into any automatically-made decision relating to your Personal Data.</li>
<li><strong>The right to object</strong> to our processing of your Personal Data.</li>
<li><strong>The right of restriction</strong>: the right to request that we restrict the processing of your Personal Data.</li>
<li><strong>The right to withdraw consent</strong> at any time where we relied on your consent to process your personal information.</li>
<li><strong>The right to complain to a supervisory authority</strong> if you consider our processing unlawful.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Legal basis for processing:</strong></p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Legal basis</th><th>Data and purposes covered</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Contract performance</td><td>Identity data, account login data, survey responses: core service delivery and transactional messages about your own account</td></tr>
<tr><td>Legitimate interests</td><td>XP/leaderboard data, usage analytics, Creator Network outreach, optional product and research messages to creators (opt out at any time)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Consent</td><td>Sensitive personal information voluntarily provided in survey responses; can be withdrawn at any time</td></tr>
<tr><td>Legal obligation</td><td>Financial records and compliance obligations</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>International data transfers:</strong> Data is stored in the United States. Uploaded images and respondent files are stored with Cloudflare in western North America. Transfers from the EEA/UK rely on the Standard Contractual Clauses (SCCs) in our data processing agreements with our sub-processors (OpenRouter, OpenAI, Stripe, Google, Cloudflare), and/or the EU-US Data Privacy Framework where applicable. Requests to AI model providers that we reach only through OpenRouter, such as Anthropic, are passed to them by OpenRouter under its own agreements with those providers. Contact hello@subo.ai for details.</p>
<p>Our service lets you directly access, rectify, delete your Personal Data or export it in a commonly used format. See "Your Rights" above for what your account page lets you do. If you are unable to perform these actions yourself, or for other requests related to exercising your rights, please contact us at hello@subo.ai.</p>
</div>
						</div>
					</div>


					<div class="text-08__item">
						<h3 class="def-18_72 title-text--inner text--08__heading">California Residents
							<div class="text--08__icon">
							</div>
						</h3>
						<div class="content_box">
							<div class="content-text"><p>The California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) and California Privacy Rights Act (CPRA, effective January 1, 2023) give California consumers certain rights over their personal information. If you are a California resident, you have the right to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Request disclosure of the categories and specific pieces of Personal Data we collected about you, and the categories of sources, business purposes, and third parties involved.</li>
<li>Delete Personal Data we collected from you (subject to certain exceptions).</li>
<li><strong>Correct inaccurate personal information</strong> we hold about you (CPRA right).</li>
<li><strong>Limit the use of sensitive personal information</strong> (e.g., racial or ethnic origin, health information, sexual orientation) that you may have voluntarily provided in survey responses (CPRA right).</li>
<li><strong>Know about automated decision-making</strong>: survey targeting in Subo is role- and rules-based, not based on machine-learning profiling (CPRA right).</li>
<li>Opt out of the sale of your personal information. As stated in this Privacy Policy, we will never disclose Personal Data to third parties in exchange for monetary consideration without your consent, and we do not sell personal information.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you reside in California and would like to exercise any of these rights, please send us an email at hello@subo.ai. You have the right to designate an authorized agent to make a request on your behalf; we will require a signed, written authorization from you to verify the authorization.</p>
</div>
						</div>
					</div>


					<div class="text-08__item">
						<h3 class="def-18_72 title-text--inner text--08__heading">Brazil Residents (LGPD)
							<div class="text--08__icon">
							</div>
						</h3>
						<div class="content_box">
							<div class="content-text"><p>If you are a resident of Brazil, the Lei Geral de Proteção de Dados (LGPD) grants you the following rights regarding your personal data:</p>
<ul>
<li>The right to confirmation of the existence of processing.</li>
<li>The right to access your personal data.</li>
<li>The right to correction of incomplete, inaccurate, or outdated data.</li>
<li>The right to anonymization, blocking, or deletion of unnecessary or excessive data, or data processed in non-compliance with the LGPD.</li>
<li>The right to data portability.</li>
<li>The right to information about third parties with whom we have shared your data.</li>
<li>The right to withdraw consent at any time.</li>
<li>The right to lodge a complaint with the Brazilian National Data Protection Authority (ANPD).</li>
</ul>
<p>The legal bases we rely on for processing your personal data under the LGPD include: consent, contract performance, and legitimate interests.</p>
<p>To exercise any of these rights, please contact us at hello@subo.ai.</p>
</div>
